Z||000000||Black86SE||12-02-2001||11:52 PM||||Is $115 plus any other needed parts charges a good price to get the valves ground and new seats on the heads of my 2.8? They said it would probably be a good idea to get at least new guides put in and some other stuff too, this would cost $250. Need some input.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Black86SEsig_1.gif">  <BR>Black 1986 SE V6<BR>Automatic - 5-speed soon!<BR>17" Motegi MR7 Wheels<BR>P235/45/ZR17 <BR>BF Goodrich g-Force KDWS<BR>AIM ScreenName: Black86se||||reg||5
Z||000001||Butter||12-03-2001||11:41 AM||||Last vavle job I had done to a in line six head, cost me less than $60, done right.  <P>There is a tolerence on the vavle guides. Vavle's with oversized stems can be ordered and woren guides can be reemed to fit.  The last option is a good idea when vavles are of marginal use.  Avoid the use of ANY sleeved vavle guide.  Make sure the person doing the vavle job seats each vavle and not just grind the vavles and seats and replace.  I've noticed in my area it becoming a standard practice for NAPA service centers to do headwork without seating the vavles and working the heads "dry" or without oil.<P>Headwork is of major importance as this will effect the smooth operation of ANY engine as well as its performance, economy, and durability.  Check with the best mechanics in your area to find the place where headwork is done right.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/buttersig_1.gif"> ||||reg||4
